{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_013i7aCY8DrQDh51W9fqq2ws","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the research project carefully to define three tiers of dream simulation scenarios.\n\nKey aspects of this project:\n1. Cross-lingual correctness probe transfer across architectures\n2. Core claims: Pareto frontier, decomposition, CaCLR method/negative result, orthogonality\n3. Target venue: EACL 2027\n4. Two main models for submission: Qwen3-235B-A22B and Llama 4 Scout\n5. 20 languages\n\nFrom MVP data:\n- EN best AUROC: ~0.73-0.75\n- Zero-shot transfer: 4-5/5 languages pass (AUROC > 0.55)\n\n\n- Best zero-shot AUROC ranges from 0.56-0.69, with mean-shift mathematically proven to be rank-invariant across AUROC values\n- ECE calibration is around 0.19-0.21, falling short of the 0.15 threshold\n- CKA analysis reveals Simpson's Paradox—raw correlations are negative but partial correlations show positive relationships (rho=+0.41, p=0.006) for Llama\n- Control tasks show selectivity between 0.02-0.26, concentrated in the deeper layers (L20-L27)\n- Layer depth differences span approximately 0.095-0.143\n\nThe critical unknowns at full scale are whether Simpson's Paradox generalizes across 20 languages (Qwen's pilot showed weaker results at rho=+0.18, p=0.274), whether CaCLR consistency loss provides meaningful benefits, if minimal probe pairs can match full performance, whether the Pareto frontier holds across languages and new architectures, if difficulty decomposition enables better transfer, whether control tasks expose capacity limitations in low-resource settings, and whether the Procrustes effect reversal persists at scale.\n\nFor outcomes, the best case would be all core findings replicating and strengthening—Simpson's Paradox significant for both Qwen and Llama, CaCLR actually improving performance, clear Pareto frontiers on both architectures, decomposition working prescriptively, linear projection confirmed, control task effects at 28%+ for low-resource languages, and a strong paper combining methodological contribution with geometric insight. The middle ground involves mixed replication—Simpson's Paradox holding for one architecture but not the other, CaCLR being inert (a negative result but still publishable), Pareto frontier clear on one architecture only, decomposition working qualitatively without quantitative prescriptiveness, and some metrics holding while others need adjustment. The worst case involves core findings failing to replicate at scale.\n\nNow I'm defining concrete metrics to track across these scenarios: English AUROC at the best layer for both models, zero-shot transfer success rate across the 20 languages, Pareto Spearman correlation between depth and transfer degradation per architecture, Simpson's Paradox partial correlation controlling for depth, CaCLR's AUROC improvement versus zero-shot baseline, linear projection saturation point, and control task artifact rates for low-resource languages.\n\nI need to be careful about the baseline numbers since the full-scale experiments use much larger models than the MVP, so absolute performance will likely shift, but I should focus on relative metrics and effect sizes instead.\n\nWriting YAML config... In the median scenario, Pareto correlation weakens on one architecture while Simpson's partial rho becomes architecture-dependent, CaCLR shows no effect, and the decomposition findings lack quantitative support. The worst case sees AUROC plateau near baseline levels, inconsistent zero-shot performance, and both statistical measures failing to reach significance across the board.\n\nLooking at resource constraints, linear projection only holds for high-resource languages while control artifacts spike above 40%, and projecting out difficulty doesn't actually shift the transfer patterns. I'm reconsidering whether the zero-shot pass rate scales as expected—with 20 languages including more low-resource ones, the improvement might be sublinear compared to the 7B baseline. The core claims hinge on whether Pareto frontier strength varies by architecture and whether decomposition meaningfully predicts transfer performance.\n\nFor CaCLR to work, I need the consistency loss weight to show a clear effect size, and the Simpson's Paradox finding in CKA analysis has to hold up—if that doesn't replicate, the geometric insight framing collapses and I lose the novelty positioning.\n\nThe Pareto frontier needs to show architecture-dependent patterns clearly, otherwise Claim 1 weakens. If CaCLR fails AND the CKA subspace explanation for why it fails also doesn't pan out, that's a compounding problem. Control tasks revealing more artifacts than expected would undermine the probe validity, and if the decomposition doesn't cleanly separate the factors, Claim 2 falls apart.\n\nEven in a worst-case scenario though, there's a fallback: I can reframe this as a negative results paper about what doesn't work for cross-lingual calibration, emphasize linear projection as the only viable method with practical characterization, highlight control tasks as a methodological warning, and conclude that probe-based calibration is fundamentally harder cross-lingually than the field assumes.\n\nNow I need to finalize the YAML and be careful about which metrics I'm actually tracking.\n\nPicking the key metrics that will tell the story: English AUROC across models, zero-shot pass rate across 20 languages, how many architectures show the depth-transfer tradeoff, the partial correlation ranges and significance counts, CaCLR's improvement over zero-shot, how many parallel pairs are needed for saturation, the artifact rate in control tasks, and the decomposition metrics.\n\nNow I need to be careful about directionality — some metrics are better when higher (AUROC, pass rates, significant architectures) while others need context. The artifact rate is tricky since a higher rate signals methodological rigor in detecting probe artifacts, which is actually valuable for the paper's contribution, even though it means lower probe validity. Let me frame each metric from what strengthens the narrative.\n\nWriting YAML config... Now I'm looking at the specific numerical ranges for each finding—English AUROC peaks between 0.77 and 0.83, zero-shot transfer succeeds across 16-18 of the 20 languages tested, and the Pareto frontier shows statistically significant architecture dependence with correlation coefficients between 0.30 and 0.55. The CaCLR consistency loss delivers a 2-5 percentage point improvement over baseline, linear projection saturates around 30-50 training pairs, and control task artifacts appear in roughly a quarter to a third of low-resource languages, with difficulty decomposition yielding modest but consistent gains for capability-limited cases.\n\nThe narrative framing keeps the geometric insight front and center—Simpson's Paradox as the novel finding, CaCLR as the positive methodological contribution that shifts the Pareto frontier, and the title emphasizing how geometric analysis reveals when and why cross-lingual calibration transfer succeeds. The four-claim structure holds: Pareto discovery, decomposition analysis, CaCLR method, and CKA geometric explanation, with control task warnings and projection efficiency as supporting contributions.\n\nFor the median scenario, Simpson's Paradox only reaches significance on one architecture, CaCLR's consistency loss shows no effect, and while the Pareto frontier architecture-dependence is confirmed, the decomposition analysis becomes qualitative rather than quantitative. Looking at the specific numerical ranges for these key metrics—the Simpson's correlation showing significance on one architecture but marginal on another, the CaCLR delta essentially at zero, and the control artifact rates sitting lower than initially expected—I'm seeing a pattern where the effects are real but much more modest and architecture-dependent than the pilot suggested. The Procrustes reversal threshold has a wider range too, indicating noisier behavior across conditions.\n\nThe narrative is shifting toward acknowledging that distributional matching doesn't actually solve the correctness probe problem, with geometric CKA analysis providing the mechanistic explanation through subspace arguments. Simpson's Paradox becomes an architecture-specific finding—reliable on Llama-class models but not replicating on Qwen-class—so the framing needs careful hedging around when the effect holds. The decomposition claim moves from prescriptive to descriptive, and the practical contribution crystallizes around the linear projection approach needing roughly 50-80 pairs. The paper's angle is becoming more about understanding when cross-lingual calibration actually transfers, grounded in empirical rigor and geometric insight, with the negative results strengthening rather than weakening the contribution for a venue like EACL.\n\nBut now the worst-case scenario is emerging: Simpson's Paradox doesn't hold up at scale across 20 languages on either architecture, the geometric explanation collapses because CKA adds no real explanatory power beyond depth, the Pareto frontier is too noisy to claim a meaningful tradeoff, difficulty decomposition doesn't actually change transfer patterns, control task artifacts contaminate over 35% of the data, and linear projection only helps inconsistently—working for low-resource zero-shot languages but potentially hurting others. The core narrative needs a major shift—instead of the geometric insight angle, I'm reframing this as a large-scale negative result about probe-based calibration failing to transfer reliably across languages. The paper becomes a methodological warning that demonstrates this through rigorous controls and statistical testing, with the main contributions being the control task analysis showing artifact rates above 35%, a comprehensive closure of both distributional and geometric alignment approaches, and practical guidance on when transfer methods actually work depending on language and resource conditions.\n\nNow I'm verifying the metric consistency across the best, median, and worst performance tiers to ensure the data integrity holds up. worst [0,1] ✓\n- simpson_partial_rho: best [0.30, 0.55] ≥ median [0.15, 0.35] ≥ worst [0.05, 0.20] ✓\n- simpson_p_value: best [0.001, 0.01] ≤ median [0.01, 0.10] ≤ worst [0.10, 0.50] (lower p = better) ✓\n- caclr_delta: best [0.02, 0.05] ≥ median [-0.005, 0.01] ≥ worst [-0.02, 0.005] ✓\n- linear_proj_pairs: best [30, 50] ≤ median [50, 80] ≤ worst [80, 150] (lower = better) ✓\n- control_artifact_rate: The tricky part here is that artifact rate doesn't follow a simple monotonic pattern with paper quality—a moderate rate actually confirms the finding while a lower rate weakens it, and a high rate undermines probe validity. But I need to respect the best ≥ median ≥ worst constraint, so I'll set best as moderate (confirms finding), median as lower (weaker finding but better probe validity), and worst as higher (undermines validity broadly). I'm going in circles on whether artifact rate should be monotonic or have a sweet spot. The real issue is that I need to pick a consistent direction: treating higher artifact rates as worse for the paper overall, since they undermine the methodological validity of the probe itself.\n\nSo I'm settling on ranges where the 28% finding from the project context actually replicates cleanly — best case around 22-32% where it stays confined to low-resource languages, and median case around 25-38% where it spreads messier across resource levels.\n\nFor the worst case, I'm pushing it higher at 35-50% when mid-resource languages get affected too. This creates a clear monotonic scale where higher rates are worse for probe validity.\n\nNow I need to handle the procrustes_reversal_threshold — instead of leaving it null, I should frame it as \"no clean threshold identified\" in YAML-compatible language. And I'm reconsidering whether \"artifact rate\" is even the right metric since it has that awkward sweet-spot problem. Maybe I should pivot to something like \"selectivity_clean_rate\" that measures what fraction of language-layer combinations show adequate selectivity, where higher is always better and more intuitive.\n\nActually, I'm going to stop overthinking this. The artifact_rate metric works fine if I just add a clarifying comment about the direction being favorable at lower rates, and I'll define the ranges clearly: best at 20-30%, median at 25-38%, worst at 35-50%. The slight overlap between ranges is acceptable. For the procrustes threshold in the worst case, I'll use a string value like \"inconsistent\" instead of trying to force a numeric range.\n\nWriting the YAML structure... Looking at the performance metrics and ranges across different evaluation dimensions—AUROC scores, zero-shot pass rates, correlation coefficients, and the number of pairs needed to reach saturation. The narrative direction confirms the core framing around Simpson's Paradox as the geometric insight, with CaCLR as the methodological contribution, and the title emphasizing when and why cross-lingual calibration transfer succeeds, maintaining all four causal claims intact.\n\nFor the median scenario, I'm noting that Simpson's Paradox shows significance primarily in Llama-based architectures from the pilot replication but not in Qwen systems—indicating architecture-dependent effects. The CaCLR consistency loss doesn't yield significant AUROC improvements across the full beta sweep, which is a negative result worth reporting. The Pareto frontier shows architecture variation exists, though language-level variance is larger than the MVP suggested, and the difficulty decomposition holds qualitatively but lacks the quantitative rigor needed for stronger claims.\n\nI'm also tracking that control task artifacts affect roughly 25-35% of cases with some impact on lower-resource languages, linear projection requires around 60-80 pairs for reliable calibration, and the resource-tier stratification isn't clean enough. Now I'm compiling the specific numeric ranges for the key metrics—AUROC bounds, zero-shot pass rates across languages, Pareto correlations, Simpson's partial correlations, and the CaCLR delta estimates.\n\nFor the narrative framing, I'm shifting CaCLR toward a negative result where distributional matching fails for correctness probes, using CKA geometric analysis to explain why this happens in architectures with steep CKA-depth gradients. Simpson's Paradox gets reported with hedged language as an architecture-dependent finding, and I'm weakening Claim 2 from prescriptive to descriptive—just observing the decomposition rather than prescribing it. The practical contribution becomes the linear projection recommendation at 60-80 pairs, and the title pivots to asking when cross-lingual calibration transfer actually works.\n\nIn the worst-case scenario, Simpson's Paradox shows no significant effect across both architectures on 20 languages, CaCLR fails entirely with no geometric explanation holding up, and the Pareto frontier is too noisy to extract meaningful depth-transfer tradeoffs.  The difficulty decomposition doesn't actually help—removing difficulty signals doesn't improve transfer—while control task artifacts contaminate over 35% of mid-resource languages, undermining probe reliability. Linear projections are inconsistent: they help low zero-shot languages but hurt high zero-shot ones, with no clear threshold separating the two regimes.\n\nGiven these findings, I need to completely reframe the narrative away from the original geometric insights. The new angle is positioning this as a large-scale negative result on the limits of cross-lingual calibration probe transfer, which still fits the EACL timeline.\n\nThe core contribution shifts to a methodological warning paper: the control task analysis becomes the strongest evidence (with artifact rates exceeding 35%), while I simultaneously close off both the distributional matching and geometric alignment pathways as failed approaches. I'll add practical guidance on which transfer methods barely work under specific conditions, and empirically refute the assumption that cross-lingual correctness direction is language-agnostic. Linear projection emerges as the most reliable baseline among the failed approaches.\n\nNow I'm verifying the monotonicity across these performance metrics... .01] ≤ [0.01,0.10] ≤ [0.10,0.50] ✓\n- caclr delta: [0.02,0.05] ≥ [-0.005,0.01] ≥ [-0.02,0.005] ✓\n- linear proj pairs (lower = better): [30,50] ≤ [60,80] ≤ [100,200] ✓\n- control artifact rate (lower = better for paper): [0.20,0.30] ≤ [0.25,0.38] ≤ [0.35,0.50] ✓\n- decomp delta: [0.02,0.04] ≥ [0.005,0.02] ≥ [-0.01,0.005] ✓\n- procrustes threshold ranges from [0.48,0.55] in the clean case up to [0.45,0.62] when noise is introduced, which shows some inconsistency but still validates. Everything checks out, so I'm ready to generate the output.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":16759,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":16759},"output_tokens":18,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7eac8e15-c451-4fd5-8ff8-bb191a26c589","uuid":"b4365dfb-fe84-44ec-99fc-32c5e0e8a77b"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_013i7aCY8DrQDh51W9fqq2ws","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"```yaml\ntiers:\n  best:\n    meaning: \"核心几何洞见（Simpson's Paradox）在双架构 × 20 语言上显著复现；CaCLR consistency loss 提供可测量迁移增益；所有 claims 完整保留\"\n    key_assumptions:\n      - \"Simpson's Paradox partial correlation 在两个架构上均显著 (p < 0.01)，CKA 控制 depth 后确实解释 transfer 差异\"\n      - \"CaCLR consistency loss (β > 0) 在深层 layer 上比 zero-shot 提升 ≥2pp AUROC\"\n      - \"Pareto frontier 架构依赖性清晰：Qwen3 强 tradeoff, Llama 4 弱 tradeoff，与 MVP 模式一致\"\n      - \"Difficulty decomposition 可处方性地预测哪些语言需要哪种 alignment 策略\"\n      - \"Control task artifacts 集中在低资源语言 (~25-30%)，不波及中高资源\"\n      - \"Linear projection ≤50 pairs 达到 95%+ 性能，resource-tier 分层饱和清晰\"\n    number_ranges:\n      en_best_auroc: [0.77, 0.83]\n      zs_pass_rate_20lang: [0.80, 0.90]\n      pareto_spearman_rho_significant_archs: [2, 2]\n      simpson_partial_rho: [0.30, 0.55]\n      simpson_p_value_max: [0.001, 0.01]\n      caclr_mean_delta_auroc_vs_zs: [0.02, 0.05]\n      linear_proj_pairs_to_95pct: [30, 50]\n      control_artifact_rate_lowres: [0.20, 0.30]\n      decomp_capability_delta_auroc: [0.02, 0.04]\n      procrustes_reversal_threshold_zs_auroc: [0.48, 0.55]\n    narrative_direction: \"原始 framing 完全成立。以 Simpson's Paradox 几何洞见为核心新颖性开篇，CaCLR 作为正面方法贡献。标题强调 'geometric analysis reveals when and why cross-lingual calibration transfer succeeds.' 四条 claim 完整因果链保留：Pareto discovery → decomposition analysis → CaCLR method → CKA geometric explanation。Control task warning + projection sample efficiency 作为加分贡献。无需弱化任何 claims。\"\n\n  median:\n    meaning: \"Simpson's Paradox 仅在一个架构上显著；CaCLR consistency loss 无效（negative result）；Pareto 架构差异存在但 decomposition 仅定性成立\"\n    key_assumptions:\n      - \"Simpson's Paradox partial correlation 在 Llama 系架构显著（复现 pilot rho=+0.41）但 Qwen 系不显著——架构依赖\"\n      - \"CaCLR consistency loss 在完整 β sweep (3 OOM) 中无显著 AUROC 提升——negative result\"\n      - \"Pareto frontier 架构依赖性存在但语言级别变异大于 MVP 所示，部分语言呈非单调模式\"\n      - \"Difficulty decomposition 定性分离成立但不具备跨语言可靠的处方性预测能力\"\n      - \"Control task artifacts 存在但波及范围扩展至部分中资源语言 (~25-35%)\"\n      - \"Linear projection 需要 ~60-80 pairs，resource-tier 分层饱和曲线不够干净\"\n    number_ranges:\n      en_best_auroc: [0.74, 0.79]\n      zs_pass_rate_20lang: [0.65, 0.80]\n      pareto_spearman_rho_significant_archs: [1, 2]\n      simpson_partial_rho: [0.15, 0.35]\n      simpson_p_value_max: [0.01, 0.10]\n      caclr_mean_delta_auroc_vs_zs: [-0.005, 0.01]\n      linear_proj_pairs_to_95pct: [60, 80]\n      control_artifact_rate_lowres: [0.25, 0.38]\n      decomp_capability_delta_auroc: [0.005, 0.02]\n      procrustes_reversal_threshold_zs_auroc: [0.45, 0.62]\n    narrative_direction: \"CaCLR 转为 negative result framing——distributional matching 对 correctness probes 失效，CKA subspace 分析解释原因（marginal matching 无法对齐低维 directional structure）。Simpson's Paradox 用 hedged language 报告为架构依赖发现（'in architectures where the CKA-depth gradient is steep'）。Claim 2 从 'prescriptive decomposition' 弱化为 'descriptive separation observed.' Claim 3 叙事按 paper_framing 条件分支执行：systematic negative result + geometric explanation 作为贡献，关闭 MMD distributional-matching 路径。强化实用贡献：linear projection ~60-80 pairs 作为推荐方案 + Procrustes effect reversal 作为 practitioner guidance。标题转向 'when does cross-lingual calibration transfer work? Geometric analysis and practical solutions.' 仍可投 EACL——thorough empirical study + 单架构几何洞见 + negative result 贡献 + 实用指导。\"\n\n  worst:\n    meaning: \"Simpson's Paradox 在 20 语言上双架构均不显著；CaCLR 失败且几何解释不成立；Pareto frontier 噪声大；高 artifact rate 动摇 probe 有效性\"\n    key_assumptions:\n      - \"Simpson's Paradox partial correlation 在两个架构上均不显著——CKA 在 depth 之外无额外解释力，5 语言 pilot 结果是 small-sample artifact\"\n      - \"CaCLR 失败且 CKA subspace 解释也不成立——correctness direction 的跨语言 persistence 弱于预期，非 robust low-dimensional subspace\"\n      - \"Pareto frontier 噪声大：depth-transfer tradeoff 多数语言无一致显著性，非单调模式普遍\"\n      - \"Difficulty decomposition 无实质效果——投影去除 difficulty 后 transfer 无变化，两信号纠缠不可分\"\n      - \"Control task artifacts 波及中资源语言 (>35%)，probe 有效性受到广泛质疑，非仅低资源现象\"\n      - \"Linear projection 效果不稳定——帮助/损害的阈值无法可靠确定，需要大量 pairs 且语言间方差大\"\n    number_ranges:\n      en_best_auroc: [0.70, 0.76]\n      zs_pass_rate_20lang: [0.50, 0.65]\n      pareto_spearman_rho_significant_archs: [0, 1]\n      simpson_partial_rho: [0.05, 0.20]\n      simpson_p_value_max: [0.10, 0.50]\n      caclr_mean_delta_auroc_vs_zs: [-0.02, 0.005]\n      linear_proj_pairs_to_95pct: [100, 200]\n      control_artifact_rate_lowres: [0.35, 0.50]\n      decomp_capability_delta_auroc: [-0.01, 0.005]\n      procrustes_reversal_threshold_zs_auroc: \"inconsistent — no clean threshold identifiable\"\n    narrative_direction: \"需要大幅 reframe，但仍冲 EACL。原始几何洞见 framing 崩塌。新 angle：'The limits of cross-lingual calibration probe transfer: a large-scale negative result with methodological implications.' 核心贡献重构为三层：(1) 领域级方法论警示——control task 分析揭示 >35% 跨语言 probing 结果是 capacity artifacts，这是该发现的最强版本，首次在 cross-lingual transfer 场景应用 selectivity 检验，对所有跨语言 probing 研究有 field-level implication；(2) 综合 negative result 同时关闭 distributional matching (CaCLR) 和 geometric alignment 两条路径，附带 component ablation 和 β sweep 数据，reproducibility bar 高于正面结果；(3) 条件性实用指导——按 resource-tier 和 baseline ZS quality 分层给出推荐，linear projection 作为唯一可行但需 100+ pairs 的暴力方案。'Correctness direction 跨语言 language-agnostic' 这一前提假设在一般情况下被经验性证伪——这本身是对 Miao & Ungar (2026) 和 CORAL 研究线的重要回应。作为 rigorous negative results + methodology paper 仍可发 EACL。\"\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":16759,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":16759},"output_tokens":18,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7eac8e15-c451-4fd5-8ff8-bb191a26c589","uuid":"b621cdcf-d2